<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#heller-coin">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heller (coin)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Haller pfennig</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Heller was a German coin first struck at the imperial mint of Schwäbisch Hall in the 12th century as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> pfennig, the Haller pfennig, from which its name comes. It spread across the Empire, gradually losing value until it was worth half a pfennig, and survived as a small coin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and, as the haléř, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heller-coin.jpg" alt="Heller (coin)" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maxda · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%A4ndelheller,_13._Jahrhundert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Heller, also called the Haller or Häller, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denarius">denarius</a> hallensis, takes its name from the city of Hall am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocher">Kocher</a>, today Schwäbisch Hall. Silver coins stamped on both sides, the Häller Pfennige, were also called Händelheller because they often depicted a hand. A distinction was made between white, red and black hellers. The composition deteriorated over time as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> was mixed in, until the coins were no longer thought of as silver. There were 576 hellers in a Reichsthaler, and the word also named a silver unit of weight equal to one 512th of a Mark.</p><p>In Swabia the heller at first matched the pfennig, with 240 hellers to a Carolingian pound, but an imperial edict of 1385 halved its value, so that 8 hellers equalled 4 pfennigs or one Kreuzer. In Bohemian Upper Lusatia the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a> coined in alternate years in the 15th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> content of the Görlitz heller fell steadily.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Because the coins were of low value and uneven quality, it was common in the High and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> to weigh large sums of hellers and to deal in the total weight, which produced amounts in pound hellers. In Electoral Saxony from 1668 the small coins called Näpfchenheller became a nuisance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a>, where people dropped them in the collection bag, and Annaberg introduced special church pfennigs in response.</p><p>The Coinage Act of 9 July 1873 replaced the old currencies with marks and pfennigs, and the heller disappeared, apart from the last Bavarian hellers, which remained valid for some time after 1878 as half-pfennig coins. The word lived on elsewhere. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> it was one hundredth of the krone from 1892 until 1918, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and its successor states the haléř or halier was one hundredth of a koruna. The Czech National Bank removed the coins from circulation in 2008. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> the haller was the lowest coin from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and survived as a nominal unit until the end of the 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the heller was a symbol of low worth. German speech keeps the idiom keinen roten Heller wert, not worth a red heller, along with eine Schuld auf Heller und Pfennig begleichen, to settle a debt in full, and seinen letzten Heller verlieren, to lose one's last heller. Ein Heller und ein Batzen is a student and soldier's song with words by Albert von Schlippenbach and music by Franz Kugler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heller (coin)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its mint produced the <b>Haller pfennig</b>, or heller, from the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-hall">Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Hall</a></li></ul></section>
